<!-- Website teaching layer for android/architecture/flow.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     stateIn/WhileSubscribed semantics per kotlinx.coroutines API (SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(stopTimeoutMillis))
     and developer.android.com "StateFlow and SharedFlow"; checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A ride-sharing screen shows the user's location three times: on the map, in a header and in a "nearby drivers" list. Each collects the same <code>locationFlow</code>. Battery usage doubles in the next release. The profiler shows three GPS listeners running at once: one per collector, because the flow started its producer again for each one.</p>

A cold `Flow` builder describes work that runs when collected, separately for each collector. That is right for one-off pipelines and wrong for an expensive source that many screens share. A common Android pattern keeps repository queries cold, shares them in the ViewModel with `stateIn`, and collects with lifecycle awareness in the UI. Other sources can already be hot. This chapter follows that pipeline and the policies that decide when upstream work starts and stops.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### Cold runs per collector; hot is shared

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Cold: <code>flow { }</code></span><div class="node"><strong>Collector A</strong><small>starts its own GPS listener</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Collector B</strong><small>starts another</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Collector C</strong><small>and another: three producers</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Hot: <code>.stateIn(viewModelScope, …)</code></span><div class="node is-key"><strong>One shared upstream</strong><small>one GPS listener in the ViewModel's scope</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>A, B and C</strong><small>read the same latest value</small></div></div></div><figcaption>Sharing is a deliberate step with an owner: the scope you pass to <code>stateIn</code> or <code>shareIn</code>.</figcaption></figure>

